      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pincher Creek earned its name in 1868 when prospectors lost a pincer in a small creek, a tool used for trimming horse hooves.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pincher_Creek"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town officially incorporated as a village in 1898 and became a town in 1906, marking its growth from a frontier settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pincher_Creek"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pincher Creek sits in a wind‑funnel where the Oldman and Castle River valleys channel strong Chinook winds, often reaching 90 km/h.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pincher_Creek"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Kootenai Brown Historical Park houses a visitor center that offers maps, brochures, and up‑to‑date details on local attractions and road conditions.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
